To diagnose the nature of this compensating structure across density classes, we decompose transport anomalies into contributions from changes in velocity and cross-sectional area for each density bin (see Methods) and construct analogous composites for each component (Fig. 2c, d). These composites show that opposing transport anomalies across densities are dominated by area-driven contributions in both the eastern and western subpolar basins. The compensation therefore primarily reflects thickening and thinning of adjacent density layers rather than compensating changes in flow speed. As a result, strong variability near \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
